{"id": "sevt_01358qBUMpTp1YwA6YoaTCtu", "input": {"evidence": "The flagged statement at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:10 builds an INSERT by concatenating `name` and `email`. Those values come only from the loop at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:9, which iterates over the module-level constant USERS defined at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:3: two hardcoded tuples, (\"alice\", ...) and (\"bob\", ...). The function seed(path) at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6 takes only a database file path. That path goes to sqlite3.connect at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:7 and is never placed in SQL. No external, request, or user-controlled data reaches the query. A repo-wide search found no references to seed_db or seed() anywhere else. The application entry point imports only Flask (app/main.py:1) and does not import the tests/fixtures module. The file is test-fixture code and is not reachable from the deployed app.", "finding_id": "F-105", "recommended_fix": "No security fix is needed. As a hygiene fix, and so the scanner stops flagging it, use parameterized queries: conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", (name, email)), or conn.executemany with USERS. You could also exclude tests/ from production SAST rules.", "severity": "none", "title": "SQL string concatenation in test fixture seed_db.py uses only hardcoded constants (not exploitable)", "verdict": "not_exploitable"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:54:37.835958Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
