{"id": "sevt_01JypjMNMyJxGMXFNyzEd99s", "content": [{"text": "I checked the finding against the code and created one ticket draft. **F-105 can't be exploited.**\n\n| ID | Rule | Location | Verdict | Severity | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-105 | SQL Injection | tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:10 | not_exploitable | none | The SQL string is built only from the hardcoded `USERS` constant (line 3), so no outside input reaches it. The file is test-only and nothing else in the repo uses it. |\n\n- **Where the values come from:** The query does join strings together, but `name` and `email` come only from the fixed list in `USERS`. The function's only argument, `path`, is the database file passed to `sqlite3.connect` (line 7). It never goes into the SQL.\n- **Can an attacker reach it:** No. A search of the repo found no other code that uses `seed_db` or `seed()`. `app/main.py` imports only Flask.\n\n**Suggested fix:** None needed for security. To clean it up and stop the scanner flagging it, switch to a parameterized query like `conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", (name, email))`. You could also exclude `tests/` from the production scan rules.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:54:43.311190Z", "type": "agent.message"}
